    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a fair point on accountability, but it sounds like you're saying retributive justice is inherently flawed or less effective. If we look at it from a purely ethical standpoint, isn't the idea of \"just deserts\"—that a person deserves a specific level of suffering or loss in proportion to their crime—a fundamental pillar of justice? Why should we dismiss the moral necessity of retribution in favor of a model that seems to prioritize the offender's rehabilitation over the victim's right to see justice served?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
